-- How a trait or implementation reads one method, and why it may stop.
-- The owner parsers are generic over the reader. A header reader can reject
-- a method (its diagnostics are reported) but never stop; a reader that
-- completes bodies can also stop at a body outside the body slice, and its
-- stop (`StopPoint`) says why.
-- `Never` is the stop type of a reader that cannot stop, so a stop the type
-- forbids has no value and no arm to handle it.


---
No value: a union whose only variant holds another one, so none can be built.
---
union Never:
	Impossible(Never)


---
What the absence of a `Never` proves, at any type.
---
pure func absurd[T](value: Never) -> T:
	match value:
		Impossible(inner):
			absurd(inner)


-- `Ok(Some(method))` is a method read; `Ok(None)` one whose header was
-- rejected; `Err(stop)` ends the owner's parse at once, and the caller
-- restores the state it started from. The two outcomes without a method are
-- not a generic union's nullary variants: those cannot be built from another
-- module (docs/issues/generic_union_nullary_variant_built_across_modules.md).
type alias MethodRead[M, S] = Result[Option[M], S]
